#heading(numbering: none, outlined: false)[Abstract]

A contended lock decides whose critical section runs next. Patel et al. showed that FIFO acquisition lets threads with longer critical sections take more lock time, subverting the CPU scheduler. Usage-fair locks such as u-SCL, which grants millisecond lock slices and penalizes overuse, and CFL, which orders waiters by virtual lock hold time, restore a fair allocation. Both, however, pay two costs. Because the served thread executes its own critical section, every fair hand-off migrates the protected data, and locality can be recovered only by relaxing fairness. Because a lock can choose only among waiting threads, a thread that is owed service but still waking up must either have the lock held idle for it (u-SCL) or lose its turn (CFL). We call these costs _migration_ and _absence_. Delegation locks eliminate migration, since a single combiner executes every request where the data resides; absence remains, as an explicit waiting policy. We present FC-PQ, a flat-combining lock whose combiner serves the requester with the least accumulated critical-section time. In redb, an embedded key-value store with a single-writer lock, FC-PQ raises Jain fairness between 1-record and 64-record writers from 0.85--0.86 under the FIFO MCS lock to 0.94--0.995, at 1.17--1.34× MCS's throughput and with 7--11× fewer cache-to-cache transfers. It ties u-SCL because redb's writers are never absent; when FC-PQ's waiters block instead of spinning, absence reappears and fairness falls as low as 0.65 for mixed transaction sizes.
